#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18433ded-ba7e-52ee-8921-d06888eea8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337467} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b9962-a78d-520c-a5f1-4696af0045af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337468} {:answer/id #uuid "d80f015c-9f57-57ff-b3b3-76968703a7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337469} {:answer/id #uuid "e373f9c7-6b4f-56c1-aa9c-8d907c144ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1-x}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337470} {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5459e367-7b1c-5808-9d42-ba9227ea2a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337471} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9f9ce59-0774-5034-a0a7-9893cebb905f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d968cc2-d41a-56be-9db0-c9b59a5e633c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82619" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|)+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7.$"}]}
